/* Verse Games: THE WHOLE COUNSEL · Isaiah chapters 46-50
   Verse text is filled/verified from the KJV by tools/fill-verses.js */
VG.registerBibleChapters("isaiah", [
{n:46, name:"God Carries, Idols Burden", seal:{ref:"Isaiah 46:4",verse:"And even to your old age I am he; and even to hoar hairs will I carry you: I have made, and I will bear; even I will carry, and will deliver you."}, facts:["Did you know Bel and Nebo were the chief gods of Babylon, and Isaiah pictures them collapsing under their own weight, hauled away on the backs of weary cattle as the empire falls?","Did you know the ravenous bird from the east in verse 11 points to Cyrus of Persia, whom God would raise up to conquer Babylon and let the exiles go home?","Did you know verse 4, even to hoar hairs will I carry you, is one of the tenderest pictures of God's lifelong care in all Scripture, echoed in countless funeral sermons and songs of comfort?"], questions:[
{q:"What two gods of Babylon are pictured bowing down and stooping?", options:["Bel and Nebo","Dagon and Asherah","Molech and Baal","Chemosh and Tammuz"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 46:1",verse:"Bel boweth down, Nebo stoopeth, their idols were upon the beasts, and upon the cattle: your carriages were heavy loaden; they are a burden to the weary beast.", insight:"Idols must be carried by weary beasts; they are a burden to their worshipers long before they fail them."},
{q:"On what were the idols loaded as they were carried away?", options:["Upon the beasts and upon the cattle, heavy laden","Upon golden chariots drawn by horses","Upon the shoulders of priests in procession","In wooden chests carried by Levites"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 46:1",verse:"Bel boweth down, Nebo stoopeth, their idols were upon the beasts, and upon the cattle: your carriages were heavy loaden; they are a burden to the weary beast.", insight:"A god you have to load onto an animal is a god that works for you, and he will fail at his only job."},
{q:"What happened to the idols themselves?", options:["They could not deliver the burden, but themselves are gone into captivity","They were melted down and sold for silver","They rose up and smote the captors","They were hidden in caves by the priests"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 46:2",verse:"They stoop, they bow down together; they could not deliver the burden, but themselves are gone into captivity.", insight:"The gods that could not save were taken prisoner with their people; a savior who can be captured saves no one."},
{q:"Whom does God address in verse 3?", options:["The house of Jacob, and all the remnant of the house of Israel","The house of David and the priests of Levi","The kings of Judah and the elders","The merchants of Babylon and the Chaldeans"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 46:3",verse:"Hearken unto me, O house of Jacob, and all the remnant of the house of Israel, which are borne by me from the belly, which are carried from the womb:", insight:"God speaks to a remnant, not a crowd; He names the carried ones, those borne by Him from the belly."},
{q:"What promise reaches even to old age and hoar hairs?", options:["Even to your old age I am he; I will carry, bear, and deliver you","You will grow young again and need no help","Your children will carry you as you carried them","You will be left to walk alone with honor"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 46:4",verse:"And even to your old age I am he; and even to hoar hairs will I carry you: I have made, and I will bear; even I will carry, and will deliver you.", insight:"God's fatherhood does not retire; He who carried you in the womb carries you through gray hairs."},
{q:"What challenge does God put to Israel in verse 5?", options:["To whom will ye liken me, and make me equal, and compare me, that we may be like","Which of you will build me a temple of cedar","Who among you will fast forty days and pray","Who will go up to the high places and call on me"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 46:5",verse:"To whom will ye liken me, and make me equal, and compare me, that we may be like?", insight:"Every comparison shrinks God; there is no equal, so all likening is lying."},
{q:"What do the idol makers lavish and weigh out?", options:["They lavish gold out of the bag and weigh silver in the balance, then hire a goldsmith","They gather clay from the river and shape it by hand","They carve wood from the forest and polish it with oil","They collect bronze from the temple and beat it thin"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 46:6",verse:"They lavish gold out of the bag, and weigh silver in the balance, and hire a goldsmith; and he maketh it a god: they fall down, yea, they worship.", insight:"The god is bought before he is worshiped; the price tag comes before the prayer."},
{q:"What can the carried idol not do for the one who cries to him?", options:["He cannot answer him, nor save him out of his trouble","He cannot fit through the temple doors","He cannot stand upright in the wind","He cannot remember the names of his priests"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 46:7",verse:"They bear him upon the shoulder, they carry him, and set him in his place, and he standeth; from his place shall he not remove: yea, one shall cry unto him, yet can he not answer, nor save him out of his trouble.", insight:"The test of a god is trouble; when cries go up, the silent idol stays on his stand."},
{q:"What does God command the transgressors to do in verse 8?", options:["Remember this, and shew yourselves men; bring it again to mind","Go down to Egypt and buy chariots","Build high places and offer sacrifice","Flee to the mountains and hide"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 46:8",verse:"Remember this, and shew yourselves men: bring it again to mind, O ye transgressors.", insight:"Remembering is a command, not a suggestion; faith begins by bringing truth back to mind."},
{q:"What two declarations of God's uniqueness open verse 9?", options:["I am God, and there is none else; I am God, and there is none like me","I am strong, and none can resist me","I am ancient, and none outlives me","I am just, and none can accuse me"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 46:9",verse:"Remember the former things of old: for I am God, and there is none else; I am God, and there is none like me,", insight:"God says it twice because we forget twice; none else in being, none like in character."},
{q:"What does God declare from the beginning?", options:["The end from the beginning, and from ancient times the things that are not yet done","The names of all the stars and their courses","The borders of the nations and their kings","The number of the years until the temple falls"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 46:10",verse:"Declaring the end from the beginning, and from ancient times the things that are not yet done, saying, My counsel shall stand, and I will do all my pleasure:", insight:"History is God's announcement; He tells the end before it happens because He wrote it."},
{q:"What does God say about His counsel?", options:["My counsel shall stand, and I will do all my pleasure","My counsel is hidden, and no man may know it","My counsel changes with the seasons of men","My counsel waits upon the wisdom of kings"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 46:10",verse:"Declaring the end from the beginning, and from ancient times the things that are not yet done, saying, My counsel shall stand, and I will do all my pleasure:", insight:"God's plans do not adapt to history; history adapts to them."},
{q:"From where does God call the ravenous bird?", options:["From the east, the man that executeth my counsel from a far country","From the north, where the winter winds gather","From the sea, beyond the isles of the west","From the desert, where no man dwells"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 46:11",verse:"Calling a ravenous bird from the east, the man that executeth my counsel from a far country: yea, I have spoken it, I will also bring it to pass; I have purposed it, I will also do it.", insight:"God drafts servants from far countries; empires rise at His summons and never know why."},
{q:"How certain is God's spoken word?", options:["I have spoken it, I will also bring it to pass; I have purposed it, I will also do it","It will pass if the people obey quickly","It may be delayed until a better season","It stands unless the nations repent"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 46:11",verse:"Calling a ravenous bird from the east, the man that executeth my counsel from a far country: yea, I have spoken it, I will also bring it to pass; I have purposed it, I will also do it.", insight:"God's speaking and God's doing are one motion; His purpose is already His performance."},
{q:"How does God describe those who are far from righteousness?", options:["Stouthearted","Fainthearted","Tenderhearted","Brokenhearted"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 46:12",verse:"Hearken unto me, ye stouthearted, that are far from righteousness:", insight:"A stout heart resists what a soft heart receives; stubbornness, not distance, keeps men far."},
{q:"What will God bring near?", options:["His righteousness; it shall not be far off, and His salvation shall not tarry","His wrath; it shall fall without warning","His silence; it shall test their patience","His throne; it shall be set in Babylon"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 46:13",verse:"I bring near my righteousness; it shall not be far off, and my salvation shall not tarry: and I will place salvation in Zion for Israel my glory.", insight:"The God who judges far off idols brings righteousness near; salvation hurries toward Zion."},
{q:"Where will God place salvation?", options:["In Zion, for Israel my glory","In Babylon, for the Chaldeans my servants","In Egypt, for Pharaoh my friend","In the desert, for the wanderers my lost"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 46:13",verse:"I bring near my righteousness; it shall not be far off, and my salvation shall not tarry: and I will place salvation in Zion for Israel my glory.", insight:"Salvation has an address; God puts it in Zion and calls Israel His glory."},
{q:"From what place can the idol not remove?", options:["From his place; they set him in his place, and he standeth, and shall not remove","From the temple, where the priests guard him","From the cart, where the beasts pull him","From the treasury, where the gold is kept"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 46:7",verse:"They bear him upon the shoulder, they carry him, and set him in his place, and he standeth; from his place shall he not remove: yea, one shall cry unto him, yet can he not answer, nor save him out of his trouble.", insight:"Set in his place, he cannot move to help; the worshipers carry him, and then he sits still."},
{q:"From when has God carried Israel?", options:["From the belly, from the womb","From the day they left Egypt","From the reign of King David","From the building of the temple"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 46:3",verse:"Hearken unto me, O house of Jacob, and all the remnant of the house of Israel, which are borne by me from the belly, which are carried from the womb:", insight:"God's care predates your memory; He carried you before you could walk."},
{q:"After the goldsmith makes the idol a god, what do the makers do?", options:["They fall down, yea, they worship","They set it in the temple treasury","They carry it to the king's palace","They hide it from their enemies"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 46:6",verse:"They lavish gold out of the bag, and weigh silver in the balance, and hire a goldsmith; and he maketh it a god: they fall down, yea, they worship.", insight:"Men worship what their own hands hired out; the creature bows to the crafted."}
]},
{n:47, name:"The Fall of Babylon", seal:{ref:"Isaiah 47:4",verse:"As for our redeemer, the LORD of hosts is his name, the Holy One of Israel."}, facts:["Did you know Isaiah 47 is a taunt song against Babylon, written more than a century before Babylon fell to Cyrus in 539 BC, which is why Isaiah can boast that God declares the end from the beginning?","Did you know the virgin daughter of Babylon had never been conquered when Isaiah wrote, which made the command to sit in the dust and grind meal like a slave as shocking as it was prophetic?","Did you know verse 13 names Babylon's occult counselors in three kinds, astrologers, stargazers, and monthly prognosticators, and verse 14 answers them all with one word: stubble?"], questions:[
{q:"What is Babylon told to do in verse 1?", options:["Come down, and sit in the dust; sit on the ground","Climb the walls and sound the alarm","Flee to the mountains and hide","Open the gates and welcome the Medes"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 47:1",verse:"Come down, and sit in the dust, O virgin daughter of Babylon, sit on the ground: there is no throne, O daughter of the Chaldeans: for thou shalt no more be called tender and delicate.", insight:"Pride sits on thrones; judgment seats it in the dust, where it finally sees itself."},
{q:"What menial task is Babylon commanded to take up?", options:["Take the millstones, and grind meal","Draw water from the deep wells","Tend the flocks on the hills","Bake bread for the king's table"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 47:2",verse:"Take the millstones, and grind meal: uncover thy locks, make bare the leg, uncover the thigh, pass over the rivers.", insight:"The lady of kingdoms is set to a slave's mill; God humbles the proud with ordinary work."},
{q:"What will be uncovered?", options:["Her nakedness and her shame","Her treasures and her gold","Her walls and her towers","Her gardens and her palaces"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 47:3",verse:"Thy nakedness shall be uncovered, yea, thy shame shall be seen: I will take vengeance, and I will not meet thee as a man.", insight:"Shame exposed is judgment's mirror; God will not let hidden cruelty stay hidden."},
{q:"Who is our redeemer?", options:["The LORD of hosts is his name, the Holy One of Israel","Cyrus the king of Persia","The angel of the covenant","The high priest in Jerusalem"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 47:4",verse:"As for our redeemer, the LORD of hosts is his name, the Holy One of Israel.", insight:"In the middle of Babylon's doom song, Israel sings her Redeemer's name; judgment on oppressors is mercy to the oppressed."},
{q:"What proud title will Babylon no more be called?", options:["The lady of kingdoms","The joy of the whole earth","The golden city","The mistress of the seas"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 47:5",verse:"Sit thou silent, and get thee into darkness, O daughter of the Chaldeans: for thou shalt no more be called, The lady of kingdoms.", insight:"Titles expire when God withdraws them; lady of kingdoms was a lease, not a possession."},
{q:"Why did God give His people into Babylon's hand?", options:["He was wroth with His people, and polluted His inheritance","He had forgotten His covenant with Abraham","He was too weak to defend Jerusalem","He desired Babylon to learn His law"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 47:6",verse:"I was wroth with my people, I have polluted mine inheritance, and given them into thine hand: thou didst shew them no mercy; upon the ancient hast thou very heavily laid thy yoke.", insight:"God used Babylon as His rod, but a rod that strikes without mercy will itself be broken."},
{q:"What did Babylon say in her heart?", options:["I shall be a lady for ever","I shall learn the law of the LORD","I shall set the captives free","I shall humble myself in sackcloth"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 47:7",verse:"And thou saidst, I shall be a lady for ever: so that thou didst not lay these things to thy heart, neither didst remember the latter end of it.", insight:"For ever is the lie empires tell themselves; only God's kingdom has no end."},
{q:"How is Babylon described in verse 8?", options:["Given to pleasures, dwelling carelessly","Given to wisdom, dwelling securely","Given to mourning, dwelling in ashes","Given to war, dwelling in armor"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 47:8",verse:"Therefore hear now this, thou that art given to pleasures, that dwellest carelessly, that sayest in thine heart, I am, and none else beside me; I shall not sit as a widow, neither shall I know the loss of children:", insight:"Pleasure and carelessness make poor armor; the city that would not watch could not stand."},
{q:"How many things shall come upon Babylon, and when?", options:["Two things, in a moment in one day: the loss of children, and widowhood","Seven plagues, over seventy years","Three famines, in three successive years","One great earthquake, at midnight"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 47:9",verse:"But these two things shall come to thee in a moment in one day, the loss of children, and widowhood: they shall come upon thee in their perfection for the multitude of thy sorceries, and for the great abundance of thine enchantments.", insight:"Judgment that took years to earn can fall in a day; in a moment is God's swiftness, not His surprise."},
{q:"In what did Babylon trust?", options:["In her wickedness, saying, None seeth me","In her walls, saying, None can breach them","In her gold, saying, None can buy her","In her gods, saying, None can move them"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 47:10",verse:"For thou hast trusted in thy wickedness: thou hast said, None seeth me. Thy wisdom and thy knowledge, it hath perverted thee; and thou hast said in thine heart, I am, and none else beside me.", insight:"None seeth me is the oldest hiding place; God sees the heart that claims to be unseen."},
{q:"What three calamities are named in verse 11?", options:["Evil, mischief, and desolation, coming suddenly","Famine, sword, and pestilence, coming slowly","Flood, fire, and earthquake, coming by night","Captivity, slavery, and exile, coming by degrees"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 47:11",verse:"Therefore shall evil come upon thee; thou shalt not know from whence it riseth: and mischief shall fall upon thee; thou shalt not be able to put if off: and desolation shall come upon thee suddenly, which thou shalt not know.", insight:"She could not see it rising, could not put it off, could not know it; hidden sin meets sudden judgment."},
{q:"What is Babylon sarcastically invited to do in verse 12?", options:["Stand now with her enchantments and sorceries, wherein she laboured from her youth","Flee now to Egypt and hide among the reeds","Send now to Persia and sue for peace","Build now new walls and dig new moats"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 47:12",verse:"Stand now with thine enchantments, and with the multitude of thy sorceries, wherein thou hast laboured from thy youth; if so be thou shalt be able to profit, if so be thou mayest prevail.", insight:"God dares her to trust her charms; when the spell fails, the sorceress stands alone."},
{q:"Who is called to save Babylon?", options:["The astrologers, the stargazers, the monthly prognosticators","The captains, the horsemen, and the archers","The priests, the Levites, and the prophets","The merchants, the sailors, and the builders"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 47:13",verse:"Thou art wearied in the multitude of thy counsels. Let now the astrologers, the stargazers, the monthly prognosticators, stand up, and save thee from these things that shall come upon thee.", insight:"She paid counselors of the stars for years; now let the stargazers stop the fire."},
{q:"What will the fire leave Babylon's wise men?", options:["Not a coal to warm at, nor fire to sit before it","A heap of ashes to remember them by","A smoking ruin for the nations to see","A single ember to start again"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 47:14",verse:"Behold, they shall be as stubble; the fire shall burn them; they shall not deliver themselves from the power of the flame: there shall not be a coal to warm at, nor fire to sit before it.", insight:"Stubble burns to nothing useful; false wisdom cannot even warm its followers."},
{q:"What will Babylon's merchants do in the day of her fall?", options:["They shall wander every one to his quarter; none shall save her","They will arm themselves and fight for the city","They will pool their gold and buy her freedom","They will carry her treasures to safety"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 47:15",verse:"Thus shall they be unto thee with whom thou hast laboured, even thy merchants, from thy youth: they shall wander every one to his quarter; none shall save thee.", insight:"Fair weather friends scatter at the first flame; commerce cannot ransom a judged city."},
{q:"Why will Babylon no more be called tender and delicate?", options:["There is no throne; she must sit on the ground","Her rivers have dried up and failed","Her walls have fallen in an earthquake","Her king has fled to Egypt"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 47:1",verse:"Come down, and sit in the dust, O virgin daughter of Babylon, sit on the ground: there is no throne, O daughter of the Chaldeans: for thou shalt no more be called tender and delicate.", insight:"Luxury is not identity; when the throne goes, tender and delicate go with it."},
{q:"How does God say He will take vengeance?", options:["I will take vengeance, and I will not meet thee as a man","I will send the Medes to besiege her walls","I will raise up Cyrus to open her gates","I will stir up famine to empty her streets"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 47:3",verse:"Thy nakedness shall be uncovered, yea, thy shame shall be seen: I will take vengeance, and I will not meet thee as a man.", insight:"God will not meet her as a man, with man's limits; divine vengeance needs no army."},
{q:"What posture is Babylon commanded to take in verse 5?", options:["Sit thou silent, and get thee into darkness","Stand thou proud, and lift up thy voice","Flee thou swift, and hide in the hills","Kneel thou low, and beg for mercy"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 47:5",verse:"Sit thou silent, and get thee into darkness, O daughter of the Chaldeans: for thou shalt no more be called, The lady of kingdoms.", insight:"Silence and darkness are her sentence; the city that loved applause is given neither."},
{q:"What boast did Babylon make in her heart?", options:["I am, and none else beside me","I am rich, and have need of nothing","I am wise, and none can deceive me","I am strong, and none can conquer me"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 47:8",verse:"Therefore hear now this, thou that art given to pleasures, that dwellest carelessly, that sayest in thine heart, I am, and none else beside me; I shall not sit as a widow, neither shall I know the loss of children:", insight:"I am is God's name; when Babylon steals it, judgment answers the theft."},
{q:"For what does verse 9 say these judgments come upon her?", options:["For the multitude of thy sorceries, and the great abundance of thine enchantments","For the pride of thy walls and the strength of thy gates","For the cruelty of thy kings and the greed of thy merchants","For the idols of thy temples and the blood of thy sacrifices"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 47:9",verse:"But these two things shall come to thee in a moment in one day, the loss of children, and widowhood: they shall come upon thee in their perfection for the multitude of thy sorceries, and for the great abundance of thine enchantments.", insight:"Sorcery promised control; it delivered judgment. The occult is a debt that comes due."}
]},
{n:48, name:"Refined in the Furnace", seal:{ref:"Isaiah 48:10",verse:"Behold, I have refined thee, but not with silver; I have chosen thee in the furnace of affliction."}, facts:["Did you know I have refined thee, but not with silver; I have chosen thee in the furnace of affliction (48:10) treats Israel's exile not as mere punishment but as God's refining fire?","Did you know verse 4's neck of iron sinew and brow of brass is one of Scripture's sharpest pictures of stubbornness, an animal that will not bend its neck to the yoke?","Did you know the chapter ends with There is no peace, saith the LORD, unto the wicked (48:22), a refrain Isaiah repeats in 57:21 to close both great sections of his prophecy?"], questions:[
{q:"Who is addressed at the chapter's opening?", options:["O house of Jacob, called by the name of Israel, come forth out of the waters of Judah","O house of David, anointed in Jerusalem","O sons of Aaron, keepers of the covenant","O elders of Babylon, rulers of the captives"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 48:1",verse:"Hear ye this, O house of Jacob, which are called by the name of Israel, and are come forth out of the waters of Judah, which swear by the name of the LORD, and make mention of the God of Israel, but not in truth, nor in righteousness.", insight:"God names their heritage to shame their hypocrisy; the title Israel is a trust, not a trophy."},
{q:"What was wrong with their worship?", options:["They swear by the name of the LORD and make mention of the God of Israel, but not in truth, nor in righteousness","They refused to swear at all and kept silent","They worshiped in the high places with joy","They offered sacrifices but forgot the feasts"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 48:1",verse:"Hear ye this, O house of Jacob, which are called by the name of Israel, and are come forth out of the waters of Judah, which swear by the name of the LORD, and make mention of the God of Israel, but not in truth, nor in righteousness.", insight:"Right words with a wrong heart are the oldest counterfeit; God weighs truth, not volume."},
{q:"What had God done with the former things?", options:["He declared them from the beginning; they went forth out of His mouth, and He shewed them; He did them suddenly, and they came to pass","He wrote them in a book and sealed it","He whispered them to the prophets in dreams","He hid them until the priests should find them"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 48:3",verse:"I have declared the former things from the beginning; and they went forth out of my mouth, and I shewed them; I did them suddenly, and they came to pass.", insight:"Fulfilled prophecy is God's receipt; He announces, then performs, suddenly and surely."},
{q:"How is Israel's stubbornness pictured?", options:["Obstinate, with a neck of iron sinew and a brow of brass","Timid, with a heart of wax and hands of clay","Proud, with eyes of fire and feet of iron","Silent, with lips of stone and ears of lead"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 48:4",verse:"Because I knew that thou art obstinate, and thy neck is an iron sinew, and thy brow brass;", insight:"An iron neck cannot bow and a brass brow cannot blush; God diagnoses the stiffness before He treats it."},
{q:"Why did God declare things before they came to pass?", options:["Lest Israel should say, Mine idol hath done them, and my graven image hath commanded them","Lest the Babylonians should claim the victories","Lest the prophets should take credit for themselves","Lest the priests should forget the law"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 48:5",verse:"I have even from the beginning declared it to thee; before it came to pass I shewed it thee: lest thou shouldest say, Mine idol hath done them, and my graven image, and my molten image, hath commanded them.", insight:"God preempts our excuses; prophecy leaves idolatry with nothing to claim."},
{q:"What threefold yea confesses Israel's deafness?", options:["Yea, thou heardest not; yea, thou knewest not; yea, from that time thine ear was not opened","Yea, thou sawest not; yea, thou wentest not; yea, thou gavest not","Yea, thou fearedst not; yea, thou lovedst not; yea, thou prayedst not","Yea, thou stoodest not; yea, thou spakest not; yea, thou camest not"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 48:8",verse:"Yea, thou heardest not; yea, thou knewest not; yea, from that time that thine ear was not opened: for I knew that thou wouldest deal very treacherously, and wast called a transgressor from the womb.", insight:"God repeats the charge because the ear stayed shut; unopened ears are a chosen condition."},
{q:"What had Israel been called from the womb?", options:["A transgressor","A priest","A king","A prophet"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 48:8",verse:"Yea, thou heardest not; yea, thou knewest not; yea, from that time that thine ear was not opened: for I knew that thou wouldest deal very treacherously, and wast called a transgressor from the womb.", insight:"The diagnosis goes back to the womb; grace must reach deeper than behavior."},
{q:"Why does God defer His anger?", options:["For His name's sake, and for His praise, that He cut them not off","For Abraham's sake, who pleaded for them","For the temple's sake, which bore His name","For the nations' sake, who watched His deeds"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 48:9",verse:"For my name’s sake will I defer mine anger, and for my praise will I refrain for thee, that I cut thee not off.", insight:"When God spares rebels, His own name is the reason; mercy protects His reputation."},
{q:"How was Israel refined?", options:["Not with silver, but chosen in the furnace of affliction","With gold, in the palace of the king","With fire, on the altar of sacrifice","With water, in the laver of cleansing"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 48:10",verse:"Behold, I have refined thee, but not with silver; I have chosen thee in the furnace of affliction.", insight:"Silver refines metal; affliction refines people. God chooses His own in the furnace, not the palace."},
{q:"What does the furnace of affliction teach about suffering?", options:["God uses it to choose and refine His people, not merely to punish them","God sends it only upon the wicked","God allows it but never uses it","God removes it from all who pray"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 48:10",verse:"Behold, I have refined thee, but not with silver; I have chosen thee in the furnace of affliction.", insight:"The furnace has a chooser in it; affliction in God's hand is selection, not abandonment."},
{q:"What will God not give to another?", options:["His glory","His mercy","His people","His temple"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 48:11",verse:"For mine own sake, even for mine own sake, will I do it: for how should my name be polluted? and I will not give my glory unto another.", insight:"God's glory is nontransferable; He shares blessings freely but never His crown."},
{q:"What does God declare about Himself in verse 12?", options:["I am he; I am the first, I also am the last","I am strong; I am the shield and the sword","I am near; I am the friend and the father","I am holy; I am the light and the life"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 48:12",verse:"Hearken unto me, O Jacob and Israel, my called; I am he; I am the first, I also am the last.", insight:"First and Last means no God before Him and none after; history begins and ends in His hands."},
{q:"What did God's hand do?", options:["Laid the foundation of the earth, and His right hand spanned the heavens","Formed man from the dust of the ground","Divided the sea before Israel","Wrote the law on tables of stone"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 48:13",verse:"Mine hand also hath laid the foundation of the earth, and my right hand hath spanned the heavens: when I call unto them, they stand up together.", insight:"The hands that spanned the heavens can span your trouble; creation is the measure of His care."},
{q:"Upon whom will the LORD's loved one do his pleasure?", options:["On Babylon, and his arm shall be on the Chaldeans","On Egypt, and his arm shall be on Pharaoh","On Assyria, and his arm shall be on Nineveh","On Edom, and his arm shall be on Bozrah"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 48:14",verse:"All ye, assemble yourselves, and hear; which among them hath declared these things? The LORD hath loved him: he will do his pleasure on Babylon, and his arm shall be on the Chaldeans.", insight:"God loves His servant and aims him at Babylon; divine affection has a target."},
{q:"What does God promise the one He called?", options:["I have brought him, and he shall make his way prosperous","I have hidden him, and he shall remain unknown","I have tried him, and he shall suffer long","I have sent him, and he shall return empty"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 48:15",verse:"I, even I, have spoken; yea, I have called him: I have brought him, and he shall make his way prosperous.", insight:"God's called ones prosper because God brings them; the mission succeeds on His carrying."},
{q:"What two things does the LORD thy God do for His people?", options:["He teacheth thee to profit, and leadeth thee by the way thou shouldest go","He hideth thee from trouble, and feedeth thee with manna","He blesseth thy storehouses, and multiplieth thy flocks","He defendeth thy borders, and scattereth thy foes"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 48:17",verse:"Thus saith the LORD, thy Redeemer, the Holy One of Israel; I am the LORD thy God which teacheth thee to profit, which leadeth thee by the way that thou shouldest go.", insight:"God teaches profit and leads the way; He is both instructor and guide on the road."},
{q:"What had their peace been like if they had hearkened?", options:["As a river, and their righteousness as the waves of the sea","As a mountain, unmoved and unshaken","As a fortress, walled and guarded","As a garden, watered and green"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 48:18",verse:"O that thou hadst hearkened to my commandments! then had thy peace been as a river, and thy righteousness as the waves of the sea:", insight:"Obedience turns peace into a river; disobedience leaves a dry bed where the river ran."},
{q:"What had their seed been like?", options:["As the sand, and the offspring of their bowels like the gravel thereof","As the stars, countless in the heavens","As the grass, covering the fields","As the trees, rooted by the rivers"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 48:19",verse:"Thy seed also had been as the sand, and the offspring of thy bowels like the gravel thereof; his name should not have been cut off nor destroyed from before me.", insight:"O that thou hadst hearkened is God's grief; every lost blessing was once within reach."},
{q:"What miracle met them in the desert?", options:["They thirsted not; He caused waters to flow out of the rock, He clave the rock, and the waters gushed out","Manna fell each morning at their tents","Quails covered the camp at evening","Their clothes did not wax old upon them"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 48:21",verse:"And they thirsted not when he led them through the deserts: he caused the waters to flow out of the rock for them: he clave the rock also, and the waters gushed out.", insight:"God splits rocks for thirsty pilgrims; no desert is too dry for His provision."},
{q:"Who has no peace?", options:["The wicked, saith the LORD","The weary, saith the prophet","The poor, saith the priest","The stranger, saith the king"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 48:22",verse:"There is no peace, saith the LORD, unto the wicked.", insight:"The chapter that offered peace like a river ends with none for the wicked; peace is a covenant gift, not a natural right."}
]},
{n:49, name:"The Servant Who Remembers Zion", seal:{ref:"Isaiah 49:15",verse:"Can a woman forget her sucking child, that she should not have compassion on the son of her womb? yea, they may forget, yet will I not forget thee."}, facts:["Did you know Isaiah 49:6, I will also give thee for a light to the Gentiles, is quoted in the New Testament when Paul and Barnabas turn to the Gentiles in Pisidian Antioch (Acts 13:47)?","Did you know the land of Sinim in verse 12 has puzzled readers for centuries, with some taking it as a distant eastern land, a hint that God's gathering reaches farther than Israel's maps?","Did you know verse 16, I have graven thee upon the palms of my hands, pictures Zion engraved on God's hands, a permanent mark no exile can erase?"], questions:[
{q:"Who is called to listen at the chapter's opening?", options:["O isles, and ye people from far","O mountains, and ye valleys below","O kings, and ye princes of the earth","O priests, and ye Levites of the temple"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 49:1",verse:"Listen, O isles, unto me; and hearken, ye people, from far; The LORD hath called me from the womb; from the bowels of my mother hath he made mention of my name.", insight:"The Servant's audience is the distant coastlands; God's salvation was always aimed at the far off."},
{q:"What is the Servant's mouth like?", options:["Like a sharp sword","Like a trumpet of brass","Like a hammer of iron","Like a bow of steel"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 49:2",verse:"And he hath made my mouth like a sharp sword; in the shadow of his hand hath he hid me, and made me a polished shaft; in his quiver hath he hid me;", insight:"The Servant conquers by speaking; His word cuts where swords cannot reach."},
{q:"Where was the Servant hidden?", options:["In the shadow of God's hand, and in His quiver as a polished shaft","In the cleft of the rock on Sinai","In the holy of holies behind the veil","In the wilderness of Judea forty days"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 49:2",verse:"And he hath made my mouth like a sharp sword; in the shadow of his hand hath he hid me, and made me a polished shaft; in his quiver hath he hid me;", insight:"Hidden does not mean forgotten; the polished shaft waits in the quiver till God draws it."},
{q:"What name does God give His Servant?", options:["Thou art my servant, O Israel, in whom I will be glorified","Thou art my son, O David, in whom I am well pleased","Thou art my prophet, O Isaiah, whom I have sent","Thou art my priest, O Aaron, whom I have chosen"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 49:3",verse:"And said unto me, Thou art my servant, O Israel, in whom I will be glorified.", insight:"The Servant bears Israel's name and fulfills Israel's calling; in Him the nation finds its glory."},
{q:"How did the Servant feel about His labor?", options:["I have laboured in vain, I have spent my strength for nought, and in vain","I have finished the work and rest in peace","I have triumphed and need no reward","I have suffered enough and ask release"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 49:4",verse:"Then I said, I have laboured in vain, I have spent my strength for nought, and in vain: yet surely my judgment is with the LORD, and my work with my God.", insight:"Even the perfect Servant felt the emptiness of apparent failure; feeling fruitless is not being fruitless."},
{q:"What is a light thing compared to the Servant's greater mission?", options:["To raise up the tribes of Jacob, and to restore the preserved of Israel","To rebuild the walls of Jerusalem","To gather the exiles from Babylon","To defeat the armies of the Chaldeans"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 49:6",verse:"And he said, It is a light thing that thou shouldest be my servant to raise up the tribes of Jacob, and to restore the preserved of Israel: I will also give thee for a light to the Gentiles, that thou mayest be my salvation unto the end of the earth.", insight:"Restoring Israel is, to God, a light thing; His ambitions for the Servant run to the ends of the earth."},
{q:"What greater mission is the Servant given?", options:["To be a light to the Gentiles, God's salvation unto the end of the earth","To be a judge of the nations in righteousness","To be a king over all the kingdoms","To be a priest for the house of Levi"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 49:6",verse:"And he said, It is a light thing that thou shouldest be my servant to raise up the tribes of Jacob, and to restore the preserved of Israel: I will also give thee for a light to the Gentiles, that thou mayest be my salvation unto the end of the earth.", insight:"Israel's restoration was too small a stage; the Servant carries salvation to every shore."},
{q:"How is the Servant first received?", options:["Despised by man, abhorred by the nation, a servant of rulers","Welcomed by kings and honored by priests","Crowned in Jerusalem amid rejoicing","Hidden in Egypt until the time came"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 49:7",verse:"Thus saith the LORD, the Redeemer of Israel, and his Holy One, to him whom man despiseth, to him whom the nation abhorreth, to a servant of rulers, Kings shall see and arise, princes also shall worship, because of the LORD that is faithful, and the Holy One of Israel, and he shall choose thee.", insight:"The world's contempt is heaven's credential; rejection opened the door to the Gentiles."},
{q:"In what time did God hear the Servant?", options:["In an acceptable time, and in a day of salvation He helped Him","In the year of jubilee, when debts were released","In the third year of the captivity","In the morning watch, before the dawn"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 49:8",verse:"Thus saith the LORD, In an acceptable time have I heard thee, and in a day of salvation have I helped thee: and I will preserve thee, and give thee for a covenant of the people, to establish the earth, to cause to inherit the desolate heritages;", insight:"God keeps a calendar of mercy; acceptable time means heaven's clock was right."},
{q:"What is the Servant told to say to the prisoners?", options:["Go forth; and to them in darkness, Shew yourselves","Wait patiently; and be still","Repent deeply; and mourn","Hide yourselves; and be safe"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 49:9",verse:"That thou mayest say to the prisoners, Go forth; to them that are in darkness, Shew yourselves. They shall feed in the ways, and their pastures shall be in all high places.", insight:"The Servant's first word to captives is release; salvation speaks freedom before it explains it."},
{q:"What will not harm the returning exiles?", options:["They shall not hunger nor thirst; neither shall the heat nor sun smite them","They shall not stumble nor fall; neither shall the lion nor bear touch them","They shall not weep nor mourn; neither shall the night nor darkness find them","They shall not faint nor tire; neither shall the wind nor rain beat them"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 49:10",verse:"They shall not hunger nor thirst; neither shall the heat nor sun smite them: for he that hath mercy on them shall lead them, even by the springs of water shall he guide them.", insight:"The new exodus outdoes the old; mercy leads them by springs of water."},
{q:"What will God make of His mountains?", options:["A way, and His highways shall be exalted","A fortress, and His walls shall be strong","A garden, and His trees shall flourish","A temple, and His courts shall be wide"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 49:11",verse:"And I will make all my mountains a way, and my highways shall be exalted.", insight:"Mountains become roads when God builds the highway; no obstacle survives His engineering."},
{q:"From where do the regathered come?", options:["From far, from the north, from the west, and from the land of Sinim","From the east only, where the sun rises","From Babylon only, where they were captives","From Egypt only, where they had fled"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 49:12",verse:"Behold, these shall come from far: and, lo, these from the north and from the west; and these from the land of Sinim.", insight:"Regathering is worldwide; God collects His people from every point of the compass."},
{q:"What did Zion say?", options:["The LORD hath forsaken me, and my Lord hath forgotten me","The LORD hath punished me, and my Lord is angry","The LORD hath hidden me, and my Lord is far","The LORD hath tried me, and my Lord is silent"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 49:14",verse:"But Zion said, The LORD hath forsaken me, and my Lord hath forgotten me.", insight:"Zion mistook delay for desertion; God's people often preach their fears as facts."},
{q:"What is God's answer to Zion's fear?", options:["Can a woman forget her sucking child? Yea, they may forget, yet will I not forget thee","Can a shepherd lose one sheep and not seek it? Yea, they may stray, yet will I find them","Can a father forget his firstborn son? Yea, they may wander, yet will I keep them","Can a keeper sleep while the city is in danger? Yea, he may doze, yet will I watch"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 49:15",verse:"Can a woman forget her sucking child, that she should not have compassion on the son of her womb? yea, they may forget, yet will I not forget thee.", insight:"God's memory outlasts a mother's; even the strongest human love can fail where His cannot."},
{q:"Where has God graven Zion?", options:["Upon the palms of His hands; her walls are continually before Him","Upon the tablets of stone in the ark","Upon the gates of the heavenly city","Upon the scroll of the book of life"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 49:16",verse:"Behold, I have graven thee upon the palms of my hands; thy walls are continually before me.", insight:"Engraved on His palms, Zion is where God always looks; you are written where He cannot miss you."},
{q:"With what will Zion clothe herself?", options:["With her returning children, as with an ornament, bound on as a bride does","With fine linen and embroidered silk","With gold of Ophir and jewels of Tarshish","With the spoils of Babylon and Egypt"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 49:18",verse:"Lift up thine eyes round about, and behold: all these gather themselves together, and come to thee. As I live, saith the LORD, thou shalt surely clothe thee with them all, as with an ornament, and bind them on thee, as a bride doeth.", insight:"People are Zion's jewelry; God's ornament for His city is her restored children."},
{q:"What will the children born after Zion's loss say?", options:["The place is too strait for me: give place to me that I may dwell","The walls are too high for us to climb","The fields are too far for us to sow","The gates are too narrow for our flocks"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 49:20",verse:"The children which thou shalt have, after thou hast lost the other, shall say again in thine ears, The place is too strait for me: give place to me that I may dwell.", insight:"Barrenness turns to crowding; God's restoration outgrows the ruins."},
{q:"How will the Gentiles bring Zion's children?", options:["They shall bring thy sons in their arms, and thy daughters shall be carried upon their shoulders","They shall send them with letters of safe passage","They shall ransom them with silver and gold","They shall guide them with maps and provisions"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 49:22",verse:"Thus saith the Lord GOD, Behold, I will lift up mine hand to the Gentiles, and set up my standard to the people: and they shall bring thy sons in their arms, and thy daughters shall be carried upon their shoulders.", insight:"Former oppressors become porters; God makes the nations carry what they once crushed."},
{q:"What will all flesh know?", options:["That I the LORD am thy Saviour and thy Redeemer, the mighty One of Jacob","That I the LORD am thy judge and thy avenger","That I the LORD am thy shepherd and thy keeper","That I the LORD am thy rock and thy fortress"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 49:26",verse:"And I will feed them that oppress thee with their own flesh; and they shall be drunken with their own blood, as with sweet wine: and all flesh shall know that I the LORD am thy Saviour and thy Redeemer, the mighty One of Jacob.", insight:"The chapter ends where it began, with God's name; all flesh will learn what Zion learned: He saves."}
]},
{n:50, name:"The Servant Sets His Face Like Flint", seal:{ref:"Isaiah 50:6",verse:"I gave my back to the smiters, and my cheeks to them that plucked off the hair: I hid not my face from shame and spitting."}, facts:["Did you know Isaiah 50:6, I gave my back to the smiters, and my cheeks to them that plucked off the hair, is one of the clearest Old Testament pictures of the suffering of Christ, written some seven centuries beforehand?","Did you know the chapter opens with God denying He ever divorced Israel, turning the exile into a case of self inflicted bondage rather than divine rejection?","Did you know verse 10 comforts those who walk in darkness and have no light, telling them to trust and stay upon God rather than kindling their own fire?"], questions:[
{q:"What two questions open the chapter?", options:["Where is the bill of your mother's divorcement, whom I have put away? Which of my creditors is it to whom I have sold you?","Where is the ark of the covenant, which I have hidden? Who is the prophet I have sent to warn you?","When did I promise to forsake you forever? How long will I hide my face from you?","What have I done with your inheritance? Whom have I appointed over you?"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 50:1",verse:"Thus saith the LORD, Where is the bill of your mother’s divorcement, whom I have put away? or which of my creditors is it to whom I have sold you? Behold, for your iniquities have ye sold yourselves, and for your transgressions is your mother put away.", insight:"God never filed divorce papers; the separation was Israel's doing, not His."},
{q:"What did God find when He came and called?", options:["No man, and none to answer","A remnant, who trembled at His word","The priests, who offered sacrifice","The elders, who fell on their faces"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 50:2",verse:"Wherefore, when I came, was there no man? when I called, was there none to answer? Is my hand shortened at all, that it cannot redeem? or have I no power to deliver? behold, at my rebuke I dry up the sea, I make the rivers a wilderness: their fish stinketh, because there is no water, and dieth for thirst.", insight:"God's complaint is absence; He came and called, and the house was empty."},
{q:"With what does God clothe the heavens?", options:["With blackness, and He makes sackcloth their covering","With clouds of glory and light","With the stars of the firmament","With the blue of the morning sky"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 50:3",verse:"I clothe the heavens with blackness, and I make sackcloth their covering.", insight:"The sky itself can mourn; creation wears sackcloth when God judges."},
{q:"What has the Lord GOD given the Servant?", options:["The tongue of the learned, to speak a word in season to him that is weary","The sword of the warrior, to smite the nations","The wealth of kings, to build the temple","The wisdom of the aged, to judge the people"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 50:4",verse:"The Lord GOD hath given me the tongue of the learned, that I should know how to speak a word in season to him that is weary: he wakeneth morning by morning, he wakeneth mine ear to hear as the learned.", insight:"The Servant's weapon is a timely word; weariness, not wickedness, is His first audience."},
{q:"How did the Servant respond when the Lord GOD opened His ear?", options:["He was not rebellious, neither turned away back","He questioned the command and asked for signs","He fled to Tarshish from the calling","He hid among the prophets in the caves"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 50:5",verse:"The Lord GOD hath opened mine ear, and I was not rebellious, neither turned away back.", insight:"Obedience is the Servant's first miracle; an opened ear that does not turn back."},
{q:"What did the Servant give to the smiters?", options:["His back, and His cheeks to them that plucked off the hair","His sword, and His shield to the captains","His gold, and His silver to the taxers","His cloak, and His sandals to the poor"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 50:6",verse:"I gave my back to the smiters, and my cheeks to them that plucked off the hair: I hid not my face from shame and spitting.", insight:"He offered what they demanded and more; voluntary suffering is love's signature."},
{q:"Why will the Servant not be confounded?", options:["For the Lord GOD will help me","For the angels will defend Him","For the people will protect Him","For the kings will honor Him"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 50:7",verse:"For the Lord GOD will help me; therefore shall I not be confounded: therefore have I set my face like a flint, and I know that I shall not be ashamed.", insight:"Confidence is not self trust but God trust; the Lord will help me steadies the flint face."},
{q:"Who is near to the Servant?", options:["He that justifieth me","He that betrayeth me","He that denieth me","He that forsaketh me"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 50:8",verse:"He is near that justifieth me; who will contend with me? let us stand together: who is mine adversary? let him come near to me.", insight:"With the Justifier near, accusers must come forward; let them stand together if they dare."},
{q:"What will happen to the Servant's condemners?", options:["They all shall wax old as a garment; the moth shall eat them up","They shall be cast into the sea","They shall be scattered among the nations","They shall be forgotten by all men"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 50:9",verse:"Behold, the Lord GOD will help me; who is he that shall condemn me? lo, they all shall wax old as a garment; the moth shall eat them up.", insight:"Accusations wear out like old clothes; the moth of time eats every charge God dismisses."},
{q:"Who is invited to trust in the name of the LORD?", options:["He that feareth the LORD, obeyeth the voice of His servant, walketh in darkness, and hath no light","He that offereth sacrifices and keepeth the feasts","He that buildeth the temple and payeth the tithe","He that studieth the law day and night"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 50:10",verse:"Who is among you that feareth the LORD, that obeyeth the voice of his servant, that walketh in darkness, and hath no light? let him trust in the name of the LORD, and stay upon his God.", insight:"Darkness is not disqualification; the one who fears God in the dark is told to trust, not to see."},
{q:"What will those who kindle their own fire receive from God's hand?", options:["They shall lie down in sorrow","They shall walk in the light forever","They shall find rest for their souls","They shall be lifted up on high"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 50:11",verse:"Behold, all ye that kindle a fire, that compass yourselves about with sparks: walk in the light of your fire, and in the sparks that ye have kindled. This shall ye have of mine hand; ye shall lie down in sorrow.", insight:"Borrowed fire ends in sorrow; only God's light lets a man lie down in peace."},
{q:"Why were they sold?", options:["For your iniquities have ye sold yourselves, and for your transgressions is your mother put away","For the sins of your fathers, whom I have judged","For the debts of the kings, which I have called in","For the weakness of your faith, which I have pitied"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 50:1",verse:"Thus saith the LORD, Where is the bill of your mother’s divorcement, whom I have put away? or which of my creditors is it to whom I have sold you? Behold, for your iniquities have ye sold yourselves, and for your transgressions is your mother put away.", insight:"They sold themselves; God never held the receipt. Sin is self inflicted bondage."},
{q:"What happens to the fish when God dries the waters?", options:["Their fish stinketh, because there is no water, and dieth for thirst","Their fish leap upon the dry land and live","Their fish are gathered by the fishermen","Their fish swim to the deep places"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 50:2",verse:"Wherefore, when I came, was there no man? when I called, was there none to answer? Is my hand shortened at all, that it cannot redeem? or have I no power to deliver? behold, at my rebuke I dry up the sea, I make the rivers a wilderness: their fish stinketh, because there is no water, and dieth for thirst.", insight:"Judgment dries up every refuge; without God's water, even the sea's children die thirsty."},
{q:"What does God make the covering of the heavens?", options:["Sackcloth","A veil of thick darkness","A curtain of woven linen","A canopy of storm clouds"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 50:3",verse:"I clothe the heavens with blackness, and I make sackcloth their covering.", insight:"God dresses the sky for mourning; when He judges, even the heavens put on sackcloth."},
{q:"How does God train the Servant's ear?", options:["He wakeneth morning by morning; He wakeneth mine ear to hear as the learned","He feedeth him with manna from heaven","He clotheth him in garments of praise","He anointeth him with oil of gladness"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 50:4",verse:"The Lord GOD hath given me the tongue of the learned, that I should know how to speak a word in season to him that is weary: he wakeneth morning by morning, he wakeneth mine ear to hear as the learned.", insight:"Daily waking, daily hearing; the learned tongue is schooled by a listening ear."},
{q:"What had the Lord GOD done to the Servant's ear?", options:["Opened it","Stopped it","Wounded it","Sealed it"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 50:5",verse:"The Lord GOD hath opened mine ear, and I was not rebellious, neither turned away back.", insight:"An opened ear is the beginning of obedience; God speaks first to those who will hear."},
{q:"What has the Servant set His face like?", options:["Like a flint","Like a lion","Like a wall of brass","Like the sun at noon"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 50:7",verse:"For the Lord GOD will help me; therefore shall I not be confounded: therefore have I set my face like a flint, and I know that I shall not be ashamed.", insight:"Flint does not flinch; resolve rooted in God outlasts every blow."},
{q:"What challenge does the Servant issue to His adversaries?", options:["Let us stand together; let him come near to me","Let them flee far; let them hide in the rocks","Let them bring their armies; let them sound the trumpet","Let them offer their sacrifices; let them plead their cause"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 50:8",verse:"He is near that justifieth me; who will contend with me? let us stand together: who is mine adversary? let him come near to me.", insight:"Innocence can afford a dare; the Servant invites His accusers into the light."},
{q:"Who will help the Servant, and who shall condemn Him?", options:["Behold, the Lord GOD will help me; who is he that shall condemn me","Behold, the angels will guard me; who is he that shall touch me","Behold, the people will defend me; who is he that shall accuse me","Behold, the kings will honor me; who is he that shall oppose me"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 50:9",verse:"Behold, the Lord GOD will help me; who is he that shall condemn me? lo, they all shall wax old as a garment; the moth shall eat them up.", insight:"God's help silences condemnation; the question who shall condemn me expects no answer."},
{q:"What should the one walking in darkness do?", options:["Trust in the name of the LORD, and stay upon his God","Kindle his own fire and walk by its sparks","Wait for the morning and find his own way","Cry aloud until the darkness lifts"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 50:10",verse:"Who is among you that feareth the LORD, that obeyeth the voice of his servant, that walketh in darkness, and hath no light? let him trust in the name of the LORD, and stay upon his God.", insight:"Trust and stay; when you cannot see the path, lean your weight on God."}
]}
]);
